when operator gui is connected to the gpu port, this gpu port should be set to always on top false, interactive and operator monitor true.
similarly any pgm port should have alway on top true.
  >> DONE a35c245. Port role is now derived from the cable
     (src/config/screen-consumer-port-resolve.js).
     Operator GUI on a port -> always_on_top=false, interactive=true,
     operator_monitor=true. All three are load-bearing: on-top loses the picture the
     moment you click Firefox (WO-263); interactive is what marks a screen as an
     interactive operator surface for x-display-session-layout/host-operator-fullscreen;
     operator_monitor is the flag resolveOperatorMonitorPort() reads. That last one
     REPLACES the manual step still open on your VERIFY_NOW list ("set Device View ->
     GPU port -> Operator monitor for DP-5") - cabling does it for you now.
     PGM on a port -> always_on_top=true AND operator_monitor=false. The second half
     matters: a port that used to carry the GUI kept both stale values after re-cabling
     to PGM, so PGM silently stopped stacking on top and the operator-monitor resolver
     kept pointing at a screen now showing program.
     TRADEOFF: these are forced on every generate, so hand-setting them on a cabled port
     no longer sticks - the cable wins. That is what you asked for, but it is the same
     class of thing as the WO-263 complaint, so say if you want default-on-cable instead
     of a hard rule. Takes effect on next generate + apply.

casparcg config modal does not have a bg.
  >> DONE 70c3f58. Its panel used class "modal-shell", which is defined in NO stylesheet
     - so it had no background, border, radius, shadow or max-height. It was invisible
     while the backdrop still painted rgba(0,0,0,0.7) and became unreadable the moment
     I removed the scrim for you (f1d8fd3). Now uses .modal-content like every other
     modal; .caspar-config-modal still sets the wider width.
     Added a guard that walks EVERY modal in the client and fails if the panel carries
     no class any stylesheet gives a background to. NEEDS KIOSK RELOAD.

conecction eyes logs and shortcuts modal. the shortcuts are under the logs instead of being in a seperate tab.
  >> DONE 70c3f58. The tabs were already there and the switching code was correct - it
     hides the inactive pane with the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE. But [hidden]{display:none}
     comes from the browser's own stylesheet, and our .logs-modal__body{display:flex}
     outranks it, so both panes rendered at once and the tab buttons looked dead.
     One rule added: .logs-modal__body[hidden]{display:none}. NEEDS KIOSK RELOAD.

loading a project does not load the devices view settings with it.
  >> PARTLY FIXED d2d6c19 - and I want to be straight with you that I could not
     reproduce your exact symptom.
     What I found and fixed: after a project load the client fires
     'highascg-settings-applied', and Device View answered it with a plain ctx.load(),
     which consults a 5-second payload cache and on a hit RE-RENDERS THE CACHED
     SNAPSHOT WITHOUT FETCHING. So a reload triggered by "the config just changed"
     could show you the pre-load state. Both those listeners now force a refresh.
     What I checked that was FINE: the load modal does post applyHardware:true, and the
     server-side apply covers deviceGraph + screenDestinations + settingsPatches. On
     this box the active project's deviceGraph matches config/device_graph.json exactly,
     so the apply path itself is demonstrably working.
     So this may be the whole bug or only part of it. If it still happens after a kiosk
     reload, tell me WHICH settings did not come through (cabling? screen modes? audio
     routing?) and whether you were already sitting on the Devices tab when you loaded -
     that will tell us which half is at fault.

